// =========================================================================
// Hourglass -- Power BI measures
//
// Paste each block into Power BI Desktop (Modeling > New measure) after
// importing the CSVs in data/out/bi/ and drawing the relationships listed in
// relationships.md.
//
// Two rules run through all of it.
//
// 1. Rates are MEASURES, never calculated columns. A calculated column
//    evaluates row by row and then gets averaged by the visual, which gives
//    every authorisation equal weight regardless of size. A measure evaluates
//    over whatever the visual is showing, so the numerator and denominator are
//    summed first and divided once. The difference is not cosmetic: a report
//    built the other way will disagree with the warehouse and the warehouse
//    will be right.
//
// 2. Division goes through DIVIDE(). The "/" operator returns Infinity on a
//    zero denominator and that renders as a number. DIVIDE returns BLANK,
//    which renders as empty -- which is the truth.
// =========================================================================


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Base measures
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Units Authorized =
SUM ( fact_authorization[units_authorized] )


Units Delivered =
SUM ( fact_session[units_delivered] )


// Only completed sessions consume authorised units. A cancellation is not
// delivery, and counting it as such is the single most common way an
// authorisation report overstates care.
//
// The `uom_resolved` filter is the second half and is easy to leave out. A
// session whose unit of measure could not be determined has `units_delivered`
// set to 0 by transform.py, so including it happens to give the same total --
// today. That is an invariant of the transform, not of this measure, and a
// measure that is only correct because of what some other module happens to
// write is a measure waiting to be wrong. Both executed engines filter on
// both flags; so does this.
Units Delivered (Completed) =
CALCULATE (
    SUM ( fact_session[units_delivered] ),
    fact_session[is_completed] = 1,
    fact_session[uom_resolved] = 1
)


// Built from minutes, not from units, precisely so it does not need to know
// each service's conversion factor -- transform.py already applied it.
//
// Filtered on both flags for the same reason as the measure above: a
// cancelled session is not delivery, and a session whose unit of measure could
// not be resolved has no readable duration. Excluding them makes this figure a
// floor rather than an estimate, which is what docs/ANOMALY.md argues for and
// what the SQL and pandas implementations both do.
Hours Delivered =
CALCULATE (
    DIVIDE ( SUM ( fact_session[minutes_delivered] ), 60 ),
    fact_session[is_completed] = 1,
    fact_session[uom_resolved] = 1
)


Session Count =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( fact_session[session_id] )


Authorization Count =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( fact_authorization[auth_id] )


Children Served =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( dim_client[client_id] )


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The headline rate
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Weighted utilisation. Because this is a measure, a card showing "all payers"
// sums both sides across every authorisation before dividing -- a 2,000-unit
// authorisation moves the number 2,000 times as much as a 1-unit one, which is
// correct. Written as a calculated column and averaged, the 1-unit
// authorisation would count just as much.
Authorization Utilization =
DIVIDE (
    [Units Delivered (Completed)],
    [Units Authorized]
)


// Clamped per authorisation, then summed -- SUM(max(0, a - d)), not
// max(0, SUM(a) - SUM(d)). With any over-delivered authorisation in scope the
// two differ, and analytics.py clamps per row. Two consumers of one warehouse
// disagreeing by a rounding-sized amount is exactly the kind of discrepancy
// that costs an afternoon to track down.
Units Unused =
SUMX (
    fact_authorization,
    VAR Authorized = fact_authorization[units_authorized]
    VAR Delivered  =
        CALCULATE (
            [Units Delivered (Completed)],
            ALLEXCEPT ( fact_authorization, fact_authorization[auth_id] )
        )
    RETURN MAX ( Authorized - Delivered, 0 )
)


// Hours are NOT units / 4. A unit is 15 minutes for 97153 and its ABA
// siblings, 45 for a speech session and 30 for a medical visit, which is why
// dim_service carries minutes_per_unit. Dividing by 4 across the board
// understates speech and medical by two to three times.
Hours Unused =
SUMX (
    fact_authorization,
    VAR Authorized = fact_authorization[units_authorized]
    VAR Delivered  =
        CALCULATE (
            [Units Delivered (Completed)],
            ALLEXCEPT ( fact_authorization, fact_authorization[auth_id] )
        )
    VAR MinutesPerUnit = RELATED ( dim_service[minutes_per_unit] )
    RETURN MAX ( Authorized - Delivered, 0 ) * DIVIDE ( MinutesPerUnit, 60 )
)


Hours Authorized =
SUMX (
    fact_authorization,
    fact_authorization[units_authorized]
        * DIVIDE ( RELATED ( dim_service[minutes_per_unit] ), 60 )
)


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Pace -- utilisation adjusted for how much of the period has elapsed
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An authorisation a third of the way through its window should have delivered
// about a third of its units. Judging it against 100% makes healthy delivery
// look like a crisis and buries the authorisations that are genuinely behind.
//
// The as-of date is the last date on which a session was actually observed --
// NOT the last date in dim_date. dim_date spans authorisation period ends, so
// it runs about six months past the final session. Taking the maximum from the
// dimension puts "today" in the future, which drives every elapsed fraction to
// 1, makes Expected Units To Date identical to Units Authorized, and silently
// collapses Delivery Pace onto Authorization Utilization. The measure keeps
// returning a number the whole time.
//
// SELECTEDVALUE first, so a date slicer still works; the fact-derived maximum
// is the fallback for a card with no slicer applied.
As Of Date =
VAR LastSessionKey =
    CALCULATE ( MAX ( fact_session[date_key] ), ALL ( fact_session ) )
VAR LastSessionDate =
    CALCULATE (
        MAX ( dim_date[full_date] ),
        ALL ( dim_date ),
        dim_date[date_key] = LastSessionKey
    )
RETURN
    COALESCE ( SELECTEDVALUE ( dim_date[full_date] ), LastSessionDate )


Expected Units To Date =
SUMX (
    fact_authorization,
    VAR PeriodStart = RELATED ( dim_date[full_date] )
    VAR Days        = fact_authorization[authorized_days]
    VAR Elapsed     = DATEDIFF ( PeriodStart, [As Of Date], DAY ) + 1
    VAR Fraction    = MIN ( MAX ( DIVIDE ( Elapsed, Days ), 0 ), 1 )
    RETURN fact_authorization[units_authorized] * Fraction
)


Delivery Pace =
DIVIDE (
    [Units Delivered (Completed)],
    [Expected Units To Date]
)


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Measure quality -- publish the coverage next to the number
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Sessions whose unit of measure could not be determined are excluded from
// every measure above. This reports how much of the data that is, so nobody
// quotes a utilisation figure without knowing what it was computed over.
//
// The naming is deliberate: "Measure Coverage" invites a glance, where
// "unresolved_uom_pct" gets filtered out of the report by whoever builds it.
Sessions With Resolved UOM =
CALCULATE (
    [Session Count],
    fact_session[uom_resolved] = 1
)


Measure Coverage =
DIVIDE ( [Sessions With Resolved UOM], [Session Count] )


Pct Sessions Excluded =
1 - [Measure Coverage]


// Bind the coverage to the visual so a card cannot show a utilisation figure
// without the caveat travelling with it.
Utilization Label =
VAR Util     = [Authorization Utilization]
VAR Coverage = [Measure Coverage]
RETURN
    IF (
        ISBLANK ( Util ),
        "no data",
        FORMAT ( Util, "0.0%" )
            & IF ( Coverage < 0.99,
                   " (over " & FORMAT ( Coverage, "0.0%" ) & " of sessions)",
                   "" )
    )


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Operational measures
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

No Show Rate =
DIVIDE (
    CALCULATE ( [Session Count], fact_session[is_no_show] = 1 ),
    [Session Count]
)


Cancellation Rate =
DIVIDE (
    CALCULATE ( [Session Count], fact_session[is_cancelled] = 1 ),
    [Session Count]
)


// Distance from the operating floor, signed. Negative means behind.
Variance To Floor =
[Delivery Pace] - 0.90


Pace Status =
VAR Pace = [Delivery Pace]
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        ISBLANK ( Pace ),  "No authorisations",
        Pace > 1.00,       "Over-delivered",
        Pace >= 0.90,      "On track",
        Pace >= 0.75,      "Behind",
        "At risk"
    )


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Role-playing date: the inactive relationship
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// fact_authorization points at dim_date twice -- once for the period start and
// once for the end. Power BI allows one active path, so period_start_key is
// active and this measure reaches the other one on demand. Without
// USERELATIONSHIP this silently returns authorisations filtered by their START
// date, which is a different question with a plausible-looking answer.
Authorizations Expiring In Period =
CALCULATE (
    [Authorization Count],
    USERELATIONSHIP ( fact_authorization[period_end_key], dim_date[date_key] )
)


Hours Unused Expiring In Period =
CALCULATE (
    [Hours Unused],
    USERELATIONSHIP ( fact_authorization[period_end_key], dim_date[date_key] )
)


// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Trend
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Hours Delivered LM =
CALCULATE ( [Hours Delivered], DATEADD ( dim_date[full_date], -1, MONTH ) )


Hours Delivered MoM % =
DIVIDE ( [Hours Delivered] - [Hours Delivered LM], [Hours Delivered LM] )


// Rolling median session length is what surfaced the source-system change on
// 2026-04-01 in the pipeline's own checks. Worth having on the page: a step
// change here is almost never a change in clinical practice.
Median Session Minutes =
MEDIANX (
    FILTER ( fact_session,
             fact_session[is_completed] = 1 && fact_session[uom_resolved] = 1 ),
    fact_session[minutes_delivered]
)
